---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — SEO Intelligence Dashboard"
  description="About this simulated SEO analytics dashboard and the technology decisions behind it."
>
  <article style="max-width: 70ch; display: grid; gap: 16px;">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About this project</h1>
    <p>
      This is a portfolio demo built as Phase 4 of the DigiAgency application portfolio. It
      demonstrates how a modern SEO analytics surface can be assembled from a clearly bounded data
      layer up to a responsive React UI, without implying access to any real client account.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 16px;">Architecture</h2>
    <pre
      class="surface"
      style="padding: 16px; overflow-x: auto;"><code>{`src/data/         Simulated dataset (seeded, deterministic)
        ↓
src/lib/          Pure processing (filter, aggregate, trend)
        ↓
src/pages/api/    Astro REST endpoints (JSON boundary)
        ↓
src/components/   React island: <Dashboard client:load />`}</code></pre>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 16px;">Honesty labels</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Demo Using Simulated Data</strong> — UI and processing are real; source data is synthetic.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every chart and KPI carries a source tag (Simulated GSC, Simulated GA4, Simulated Clarity).
      </li>
      <li>Header banner on every page restates the simulated-data classification.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 16px;">Why this stack</h2>
    <p>
      Astro handles SEO, metadata, sitemap, and robots for free while keeping client-side JavaScript
      minimal. The dashboard itself runs as a single React island. Replacing the simulated adapters
      with real GSC / GA4 / Clarity ingestion later is a localized change in <code>src/data/</code> —
      the processing and UI layers do not need to change.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
